[{"sha":"455d5b5a3bc8af1ce3d2e6b7245e3b30afb5a883","node_id":"C_kwDOQeeko9oAKDQ1NWQ1YjVhM2JjOGFmMWNlM2QyZTZiNzI0NWUzYjMwYWZiNWE4ODM","commit":{"author":{"name":"skygazer42","email":"103022245+skygazer42@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-17T09:35:33Z"},"committer":{"name":"skygazer42","email":"103022245+skygazer42@users.noreply.github.com","date":"2026-08-17T09:35:33Z"},"message":"Keep heavyweight Docling outside the core runtime\n\nRoute Docling parsing through the stable Docling Serve v1 API and expose an optional pinned CPU service profile. The API and worker now keep only a lightweight HTTP adapter, while settings, diagnostics, documentation, and the frontend describe and configure the external service.\n\nConstraint: Docling Serve and its model stack add roughly 4.98 GB and must not inflate the MimirQ API/worker image or host venv.\nRejected: Keep in-process Docling as the default runtime | lazy model downloads remain proxy-sensitive and make deployments nondeterministic.\nConfidence: high\nScope-risk: moderate\nReversibility: clean\nDirective: Keep Docling packages out of requirements.txt; update the pinned sidecar and REST contract together when upgrading.\nTested: 3099 backend tests, 275 frontend tests, Ruff, TypeScript, ESLint, Compose config, OpenAPI coverage, official v1.28.0 image manifest, live health check, real multilingual PDF conversion, and ParserFactory no-fallback smoke.\nNot-tested: GPU Docling images and sustained concurrent conversion load.","tree":{"sha":"3312dccf11cb8501942477590ff056da2422540f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/skygazer42/MimirQ/git/trees/3312dccf11cb8501942477590ff056da2422540f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/skygazer42/MimirQ/git/commits/455d5b5a3bc8af1ce3d2e6b7245e3b30afb5a883","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":false,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"payload":null,"verified_at":null}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/skygazer42/MimirQ/commits/455d5b5a3bc8af1ce3d2e6b7245e3b30afb5a883","htm